           Procedure/function call stack layouts for I80386 encoder

For the function:

function a(b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i: integer; j, k, l, m, n, o, p, q, r, s: real)
          : integer;

      ~             ~
      |             |
	  ---------------
	  |      s      |
	  ---------------
	  |      r      |
	  --------------- -> Start of FPU parameter gallery
	  |      i      |
	  ---------------
	  |      h      |
	  --------------- -> Start of standard parameter gallery
	  |    Return   |
      |   address   |
	  ---------------
EBP ->|   Main EBP  |
      ---------------
      |   Main EBP  |
	  ---------------
	  |    A EBP    |
	  ---------------
	  |	 A Locals	|
      |				|
	  ~				~
	  ~				~
	  |             |
	  ---------------
	  |   EFLAGS    |
	  ---------------
	  |  A Result	|
	  --------------- -> Start of register saves
	  |  B -> EDI   |
	  ---------------
	  |  C -> ESI   |
	  ---------------
	  |  D -> EDX   |
	  ---------------
	  |  E -> ECX   |
	  ---------------
	  |  F -> EBX   |
	  ---------------
      |  G -> EAX   |
	  --------------- -> Start of FPU saves
	  |  J -> ST(0) |
	  ---------------
	  |  K -> ST(1) |
	  ---------------
	  |  L -> ST(2) |
	  ---------------
	  |  M -> ST(3) |
	  ---------------
	  |  N -> ST(4) |
	  ---------------
	  |  O -> ST(5) |
	  ---------------
	  |  P -> ST(6) |
	  ---------------
ESP-> |  Q -> ST(7) |
	  ---------------

Notes:

1. The offset to locals should not have changed from the check encoder.

2. The result can be 32 bits, or 64 bits if the result is real.

3. There will be no result for a procedure.

4. From 1-6 parameters (NOT real) can be found below the result or
flags (in case of procedure). Registers are only saved if they are
used in the routine or are occupied by parameters. The registers are
saved in reverse order to preserve the rule that tagged pointers are found
address-length ascending order in memory. This means that there can be a
gap in the register save sequence that must be compensated for.

5. From 1 to 8 real parameters, in full 64 bit, can be found below the standard
parameter area. The overflow real parameters will be temp pointers ontop
the normal stack.

6. The display is only an example, it depends on nesting level. Shown
is the case for a function at the main level.